{
  "sourceId": "2017-P1-Q04",
  "source": "<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/07125230/TMUA-2017-paper-1.pdf#page=6\">TMUA 2017 · Paper 1 · Question 4</a> · <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/07125231/TMUA-2017-paper-1-worked-answers.pdf#page=7\">Official worked answer</a>",
  "label": "TMUA 2017 · Paper 1 · Question 4",
  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA 2017 · Paper 1 · Question 4 — Find the parameter giving remainder 24 on division by x+1.\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E"
  ],
  "correct": "B",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Use the remainder theorem",
      "body": "<p>For division by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the remainder is the value of the entire polynomial at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. You can keep its product form.</p>",
      "recap": "A remainder at a linear divisor is found by one substitution.",
      "pitfall": "The remainder is 24, so the substituted value is not zero.",
      "pause": "Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow></math> without expanding the product.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b4-l02",
          "reminder": "A remainder at a linear divisor is found by one substitution."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The remainder theorem says that division by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> has remainder <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p>Evaluate directly: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Equating this to 24 gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, so <strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, option B.</strong></p><p>Checking <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow></math>. Expanding the full cubic is unnecessary because substitution respects its product form.</p>",
